"""Teaching text tied to this project's implemented choices and saved results."""
CONCEPTS = [
('segmentation','Segmentation versus classification','Foundations','Classification gives a label to an image. Segmentation assigns labels to individual pixels, so it tells us where a defect is.','Our output has four 128 × 800 maps, rather than one image-level label.','This is semantic segmentation: each output pixel describes a defect category. We do not distinguish separate instances of the same defect.'),
('mask','Mask and image–class pair','Foundations','A binary mask is a grid of zeros and ones aligned with the image. An image–class pair is one image considered for one defect category.','Each strip has four masks. One clean strip contributes four empty pairs. There are 45,572 pairs in the full training CSV.','A zero at a pixel means that this particular defect is absent there. Four all-zero target masks represent a completely clean image.'),
('tensor','Tensor, batch and BCHW','Foundations','A tensor is a multidimensional array. BCHW means batch size, channels, height and width.','Final input shape is (B, 1, 128, 800); output logits are (B, 4, 128, 800). M2/M4 use three RGB input channels.','The output channel dimension represents four defect classes. Intermediate feature channels are learned patterns, not extra defect categories.'),
('rle','Run-Length Encoding','Foundations','RLE replaces a long sequence of binary pixels with the starts and lengths of its positive runs.','Annotations use 1-based positions and column-major traversal. A pixel at zero-based (r,c) has position r + c×128 + 1.','I decode RLE into masks for training, then encode predicted binary masks back into RLE for the CSV. Row-major traversal would put pixels in the wrong locations.'),
('imbalance','Class and pixel imbalance','Foundations','Some targets occur much less often than others. Pixel imbalance is stronger when defects occupy a tiny part of an image.','Defect B appears in 313 images, while defect C appears in 4,100. About 84.2% of image–class pairs are empty.','A model can learn background and look good on naive accuracy or empty-aware Dice. I check foreground overlap and empty-target behavior separately.'),
('parameters','Parameters versus hyperparameters','Foundations','Parameters are numbers learned from data, such as convolution weights. Hyperparameters are choices we set, such as learning rate and batch size.','The final models have 1,096,252; 24,430,532; and 4,477,632 trainable parameters. Batch size is 16.','More parameters provide more capacity, but do not guarantee a better score. Architecture, initialization, data and optimization all matter.'),
('convolution','Convolution and kernel','Networks','A convolution slides a small learned filter across an image or feature map. The same weights are reused at different positions.','The custom blocks contain two 3×3 convolutions. The final custom head uses a 1×1 convolution to turn 24 feature channels into four logits.','Convolutions learn local patterns such as edges and textures. Shared filters make them efficient and approximately translation-equivariant.'),
('features','Feature map and receptive field','Networks','A feature map records where a learned pattern is active. A unit’s receptive field is the input region that can influence it.','Channels increase as the custom encoder goes 24 → 48 → 96 → 192 while resolution falls.','Deeper features combine local patterns into larger-context features. A channel count of 96 does not mean that we have 96 defect classes.'),
('relu','ReLU and activation','Networks','An activation introduces nonlinearity. ReLU keeps positive values and replaces negative values with zero.','Custom convolution blocks apply BatchNorm and ReLU after each convolution. Sigmoid is used separately for final class probabilities.','Without nonlinearities, a chain of linear layers would still behave like one linear transformation.'),
('encoder','Encoder','Networks','The encoder turns image pixels into increasingly abstract feature maps, usually at lower spatial resolution. Think of it as learning what visual patterns are present.','Our encoders are custom convolution blocks, ResNet34 and EfficientNet-B0. Their last maps have different channel counts and resolutions.','The encoder extracts context. It does not by itself return a full-size defect mask; the decoder must recover spatial predictions.'),
('decoder','Decoder','Networks','The decoder takes encoded features and builds a dense prediction at useful spatial resolution. Think of it as using the learned context to decide where each class belongs.','The two U-Nets upsample and concatenate encoder features. FPN builds and merges a top-down feature pyramid.','A decoder restores resolution, but upsampling alone cannot recover all lost detail. Skip or lateral features provide additional localization information.'),
('pool','Pooling and downsampling','Networks','Downsampling makes a feature map smaller. Max pooling keeps the largest value in each local region.','The custom U-Net uses 2×2 max pooling three times: 128×800 → 64×400 → 32×200 → 16×100.','Lower resolution reduces computation and expands the effective context, but loses fine spatial detail.'),
('upsampling','Upsampling','Networks','Upsampling increases the spatial size of a feature map. Interpolation fills in values from nearby positions.','The custom decoder uses bilinear interpolation to each skip map’s exact size. The pretrained-library decoders use their configured upsampling operations.','Upsampling does not create new ground-truth detail. Learned convolutions and encoder connections refine the larger feature map.'),
('skip','U-Net skip connection','Networks','A U-Net skip carries an encoder feature map across to a decoder stage at matching resolution.','The custom decoder concatenates 192 upsampled channels with 96 skip channels, giving 288 before its first decoder block.','Concatenation preserves both sets of features as separate channels. The following convolutions learn how to combine them.'),
('residual','ResNet residual connection','Networks','A residual block adds its input to a transformed version of that input. The block can learn a correction rather than an entirely new representation.','ResNet34 supplies residual blocks inside its encoder. The U-Net decoder adds a separate family of long encoder-to-decoder skips.','A residual addition and a U-Net concatenation are not the same operation. Addition requires compatible shapes; concatenation increases the channel count.'),
('unet','U-Net','Networks','U-Net is an encoder–decoder segmentation design with connections between corresponding encoder and decoder resolutions.','We use a custom U-Net and an SMP U-Net with a ResNet34 encoder. Their channel counts and number of decoder stages differ.','U-Net combines context from a low-resolution representation with localization details from higher-resolution encoder maps.'),
('fpn','Feature Pyramid Network','Networks','FPN builds a top-down set of features at multiple scales and combines them with lateral encoder features.','EfficientNet-B0 supplies the last four feature scales. Our FPN uses 128 pyramid channels and 64 segmentation channels, then produces four full-resolution logits.','The lateral paths use channel projections so features can be added. Our FPN merges aligned segmentation features by addition, rather than U-Net-style concatenation.'),
('transfer','Transfer learning and fine-tuning','Networks','Transfer learning starts from weights learned on a different task. Fine-tuning updates those weights on the new task.','ResNet34 and EfficientNet-B0 use ImageNet encoder weights; their decoders are for segmentation. All parameters are passed to the optimizer.','I did not freeze the encoders in final training. Pretraining provides an initial feature representation, not a guarantee of good segmentation.'),
('grayscale','Adapting RGB pretraining to grayscale','Networks','A grayscale image has one input channel; RGB has three. A pretrained first convolution must be adapted to the new channel count.','SMP 0.5.0 sums the three pretrained input-channel weight slices when in_channels=1. Final image normalization is (x−0.5)/0.25 after dividing by 255.','Input normalization and channel count must match between training and inference. Repeating grayscale three times would be a different implemented input path.'),
('logits','Logits','Networks','A logit is an unrestricted real-valued model output before the probability transformation. Zero corresponds to sigmoid probability 0.5.','All final segmentation heads return logits. We use BCEWithLogits for the BCE term and sigmoid when computing probabilities.','Do not threshold a raw logit at a probability threshold such as 0.35. Convert it with sigmoid, or convert the probability threshold into logit space.'),
('sigmoid','Sigmoid versus softmax','Networks','Sigmoid maps each logit independently to a number between 0 and 1. Softmax makes values across a channel group sum to one.','We have four binary defect channels and no explicit background channel. All four can be absent at a background pixel.','A five-class softmax with a background class is another possible formulation. Our actual implementation uses four sigmoid probabilities and binary target channels.'),
('normalization','Input normalization versus BatchNorm','Networks','Input normalization rescales image values. BatchNorm normalizes internal feature channels using learned affine parameters and statistics.','Final inputs use fixed (x−0.5)/0.25 normalization. The final custom U-Net uses BatchNorm inside blocks; the original small baseline does not.','These are separate operations at different points in the network. Changing input normalization at inference changes what the trained model receives.'),
('batchnorm','BatchNorm and evaluation mode','Networks','BatchNorm behaves differently in training and evaluation: it uses batch statistics while learning and running statistics for evaluation.','The final custom model and ResNet encoder contain BatchNorm. Inference calls model.eval() to use the learned evaluation behavior.','Calling no_grad or inference_mode alone does not switch BatchNorm or dropout into evaluation mode. I also need model.eval().'),
('epoch','Epoch','Learning','An epoch is one complete traversal of the training loader’s configured samples or draws. It is not one weight update.','The final runs use 9,114 draws per epoch and batch size 16, giving 570 optimizer steps. All three final runs completed 25 epochs.','Because training uses replacement sampling, an epoch can repeat some images and omit others. The draw count is fixed, not unique-image coverage.'),
('batch','Batch, iteration and optimizer step','Learning','A batch is a group of examples. An iteration processes a batch. An optimizer step changes the weights.','In the final loop, every batch produces one optimizer step. The last batch has 10 images because 9,114 is not divisible by 16.','With gradient accumulation, several iterations can feed one optimizer step. Our final loop does not use that accumulation.'),
('forward','Forward pass, loss, backward pass','Learning','The forward pass computes predictions. The loss compares them with targets. The backward pass computes how each parameter affects that loss.','The real loop is zero_grad → autocast forward and loss → scaled backward → unscale and clip → optimizer step.','The optimizer updates weights using gradients. Validation only evaluates the frozen current model and does not perform backward or optimizer updates.'),
('bce','Binary cross-entropy loss','Learning','BCE penalizes wrong binary probabilities at individual pixels. Confident wrong predictions receive a large penalty.','We use binary_cross_entropy_with_logits for numerical stability. It receives raw logits, not probabilities that have already been passed through sigmoid.','BCE learns both foreground and background pixel decisions. It is combined with Dice because overlap and sparsity also matter for this task.'),
('dice-loss','Soft Dice loss','Learning','Soft Dice uses continuous probabilities to measure differentiable overlap. Its loss is one minus that overlap.','The final loss averages the Dice term over positive image–class pairs, then combines it equally with BCE. Empty pairs still contribute through BCE.','Thresholding during the training loss would discard the smooth probability signal. We threshold later for evaluation and inference.'),
('gradient','Gradient and backpropagation','Learning','A gradient tells us how a small change in a parameter would change the loss. Backpropagation computes these derivatives efficiently through the network.','scaler.scale(loss).backward() computes gradients. We unscale them before clipping to a maximum norm of 1.0.','A gradient is not itself the new weight. AdamW combines it with its accumulated statistics to choose the update.'),
('adamw','AdamW and weight decay','Learning','AdamW adapts updates using running estimates of gradients and squared gradients, with decoupled weight decay.','All final models use AdamW with weight_decay=0.0001. Scratch starts at LR 0.001; pretrained models at 0.0003.','Weight decay discourages large weights through a separate update term. It is not the same thing as dropping activations or increasing the training set.'),
('lr','Learning rate and scheduler','Learning','The learning rate controls update size. A scheduler changes it over training. Large updates can be unstable; very small updates can learn slowly.','Final training uses epoch-based cosine decay. M4 R1 instead uses a step-based warmup/cosine schedule with a 60-step warmup.','The scheduler’s units must match when I call step(). A step-based schedule called once per epoch barely advances and can leave the model in warmup.'),
('checkpoint','Checkpoint and early stopping','Learning','A checkpoint saves model weights at a particular training point. Early stopping stops after enough non-improving validation checks.','Final selection saves the lowest tuning-loss checkpoint, with improvement threshold 0.0001 and patience 7. EfficientNet’s selected epoch is 19 even though it trained for 25.','Best checkpoint and last checkpoint can differ. I restore selected weights before scoring or inference.'),
('overfit','Underfitting versus overfitting','Learning','Underfitting means the model has not learned useful task structure. Overfitting means fitting training patterns that do not generalize well.','M4 losses fell, but foreground Dice stayed zero and scores remained near the all-empty baseline. That supports foreground underfitting, not useful convergence.','A falling training loss alone does not identify overfitting. I inspect held-out loss, the task metric and actual foreground predictions.'),
('augmentation','Training augmentation','Learning','Augmentation changes training examples to improve robustness. A geometric transform must also move the target mask.','Training uses joint horizontal/vertical flips and image-only brightness/contrast changes. Tuning and audit inputs are not randomly augmented.','Flipping only the image would misalign it with its target. Brightness changes apply only to the image because a binary mask represents labels, not intensity.'),
('sampler','Weighted sampling','Learning','A weighted sampler changes how often examples are drawn, rather than changing their labels or directly changing the loss.','Final weights are tempered per-class prevalence weights. M4 R2 uses training-only combination-group square-root weights capped at five.','Sampling rare defects more often can change the training prior and duplicate exposure. I check whether foreground improves and whether clean-target false alarms change.'),
('amp','Automatic mixed precision','Learning','AMP runs suitable operations in lower precision while retaining numerical safeguards. It reduces activation-memory cost and can speed GPU computation.','Final CUDA training uses autocast and GradScaler. FP32 weights and optimizer state do not simply become half-sized.','Gradient scaling helps avoid tiny FP16 gradients underflowing. I unscale before gradient clipping, then step and update the scaler.'),
('accumulation','Gradient accumulation','Learning','Accumulation collects gradients from several micro-batches before a single weight update. It approximates a larger batch under suitable conditions.','M4 Q6 uses a micro-batch of eight and four accumulation steps: effective batch 32. That is a calculation exercise, not the final training loop.','Divide each micro-batch loss by the accumulation count when averaging gradients. Step the optimizer and an update-based scheduler only after the accumulation window.'),
('split','Train, tuning, audit and hidden test','Evaluation','Training learns weights. Tuning selects checkpoints and settings. Audit reports a frozen choice. Hidden test labels remain unavailable to us.','The final split is 9,114 train, 1,139 tune and 1,140 audit images. The original outer holdout was split into tune and audit.','Audit must not select a model after I inspect its ranking. Earlier milestone use of the outer holdout limits the claim that this audit is entirely unseen across the project.'),
('dice','Foreground Dice and pixel F1','Evaluation','Foreground Dice averages overlap only on targets that actually contain defects. Pixel F1 accumulates pixel-level TP, FP and FN before applying the F1 formula.','Final score code calculates class-level metrics and then averages classes. Its audit macro pixel F1 is not the Kaggle public score.','Averages over pairs and ratios from globally pooled pixel counts need not match, even though the underlying binary Dice/F1 formula is the same.'),
('false-positive','FalsePositiveScore and EmptyAccuracy','Evaluation','FalsePositiveScore penalizes the fraction of predicted pixels on empty targets. EmptyAccuracy checks whether an empty target has no predicted pixels at all.','A single tiny speckle slightly changes FalsePositiveScore but turns an otherwise correct empty pair into an EmptyAccuracy failure.','These components measure different costs. Removing small false-positive components can noticeably improve empty accuracy while barely changing false-positive pixel mass.'),
('macro','Macro averaging and accuracy','Evaluation','Macro averaging gives each class equal weight. Pixel accuracy is the fraction of correct pixel decisions and can be dominated by background.','Final score averages per-class assessment scores. The ensemble audit pixel accuracy is about 99.5%, but rare defects can still be missed.','I do not interpret 0.67008 as 67% pixel accuracy. It is the recorded public competition score with its own weighted evaluation.'),
('threshold','Probability threshold','Evaluation','A threshold converts a continuous probability map into a binary mask. A lower threshold often increases detections and false positives.','Final thresholds are 0.35 for all four classes, chosen on tune data. The actual comparison is >=; milestone exercises use strict >.','I freeze the comparison rule with the threshold. A probability exactly on the boundary can change the mask if I switch between > and >=.'),
('components','Connected components and minimum area','Evaluation','Connected components are groups of neighboring positive pixels. A minimum-area filter removes whole components that are too small.','Final code uses scipy.ndimage.label with its default 4-connectivity. M5 explicitly asks for 8-connectivity. Final class areas are 32,128,128,128.','Connectivity changes which pixels belong to one component. The filter can remove genuine small defects as well as noise, so I measure that cost.'),
('tta','Test-time augmentation','Delivery','TTA predicts transformed views of the same image, inverse-transforms their outputs, then averages aligned probabilities. It does not train new weights.','We compared a selected single model with horizontal-flip TTA. The chosen final ensemble has no TTA.','If I forget to undo a flip, I average predictions from different physical pixels. Threshold after alignment and averaging, not before.'),
('ensemble','Probability ensemble','Delivery','An ensemble combines multiple trained models. Soft averaging keeps probabilities; hard voting thresholds each model first. These are different operations.','The final ensemble averages the three models with equal weights, then applies frozen thresholds and area filters.','Averaging logits is not the same as averaging sigmoid probabilities. Our implementation averages probabilities, with the recorded float16-cache / float32-sum precision path.'),
('selection','Model selection and leakage','Evaluation','Selection chooses a model or setting using permitted tuning information. Leakage occurs when supposedly held-out information influences that choice.','The ensemble won tuning. Horizontal TTA later scored higher on audit, but we did not switch to it after seeing that result.','The audit ranking is diagnostic evidence. It does not prove that the chosen ensemble is universally better, and using it to reselect would consume the audit as another tuning set.'),
('eval','eval(), no_grad and inference_mode','Delivery','eval() changes training-specific layer behavior. no_grad and inference_mode stop gradient tracking; inference_mode also removes some additional autograd bookkeeping.','Segmenter loads the checkpoint, calls eval(), and decorates prediction with torch.inference_mode().','I need both correct evaluation-layer behavior and disabled gradient tracking. One is not a replacement for the other.'),
('onnx','ONNX and browser inference','Delivery','ONNX stores a model graph and its weights in a portable format. An inference runtime executes that graph without running the Python training code.','The deployed static Hugging Face app runs ONNX Runtime Web. Exported logits were checked against PyTorch outputs.','Exporting is not retraining. Matching preprocessing, model outputs, probability precision and post-processing is still necessary to reproduce the masks.'),
('wandb','Weights & Biases','Delivery','W&B records experiment configuration, histories and results. It helps compare runs and trace which setup produced a result.','Actual offline Kaggle logs were synced to the project. Eight histories were freshly verified against CSVs for this guide; the original M1 live run could not be fetched.','Experiment tracking does not improve the model by itself. The history is evidence of a run, and its configuration must agree with the code and saved checkpoint.'),
('reproducibility','Seeds and reproducibility','Delivery','Seeds control pseudorandom choices such as initialization, shuffling and augmentation. Reproducibility also depends on splits, versions, hardware and data order.','Final split seeds are 42 and 43; checkpoints, split IDs, histories and configs are saved. M4 compares seeds 42 and 7.','The same seed is not an unconditional promise of bit-identical results on different hardware. Saved artifacts and explicit implementation details are stronger evidence than a seed alone.')
]

QUIZ = [
('foundation','What is the task in one sentence?','Given a 128×800 grayscale industrial strip, predict four pixel-level defect masks. This is semantic segmentation, so the output tells us locations rather than only an image label.'),
('foundation','What does (16, 4, 128, 800) mean?','Sixteen images in a batch, four output defect channels, height 128 and width 800. Those four channels are logits or probabilities before the threshold; a binary target has the same four-channel geometry.'),
('foundation','Why are there 45,572 pairs but only 11,393 images?','Each image is assessed for four defect classes. Multiplying 11,393 by four gives 45,572 image–class targets. One image can contribute several positive pairs or four empty pairs.'),
('foundation','How would you decode RLE start 2, length 3?','Subtract one from the 1-based start, mark flattened positions 2 through 4, then reshape in column-major order. The same numbers reshaped row-major would locate different pixels.'),
('models','What does the encoder do, and what does the decoder do?','The encoder builds visual features and context at lower resolution. The decoder uses these features and higher-resolution connections to produce dense spatial outputs. Neither word implies that this is a text-generation encoder–decoder.'),
('models','Trace one shape through the custom U-Net.','Input 1×128×800 becomes 24×128×800, then 48×64×400, 96×32×200 and 192×16×100. Upsampling and skips lead to 96×32×200, 48×64×400 and 24×128×800. A 1×1 head gives four logits per pixel.'),
('models','Why does the first custom decoder block receive 288 channels?','Its upsampled bottleneck contributes 192 channels and the matching encoder skip contributes 96. Concatenation gives 192+96=288 channels, which the decoder block reduces to 96.'),
('models','How is a ResNet residual different from a U-Net skip?','A residual adds a block input to its transformed output inside ResNet. A U-Net skip carries an encoder feature map to a decoder stage, where our U-Nets concatenate it. They differ in location and operation.'),
('models','What makes FPN different from the U-Net decoder?','FPN constructs a top-down feature pyramid, projects lateral features into a common channel width and adds them. It aligns and merges features from several scales. The U-Net decoders instead concatenate encoder skips stage by stage.'),
('models','Were the pretrained encoders frozen?','No. The final optimizer receives model.parameters() without a freezing step. The ImageNet encoders are fine-tuned along with the segmentation decoders.'),
('models','Why not use a four-class softmax?','The implementation has four binary defect channels and represents background when all are absent. A four-class softmax would force probabilities to sum to one among defects. A five-class model including background is another possible design, but not this implementation.'),
('training','Are 25 epochs the same as 25 weight updates?','No. At batch size 16 and 9,114 draws, there are 570 updates per final epoch. Twenty-five epochs correspond to 14,250 updates when each batch steps once. Replacement sampling can repeat image IDs.'),
('training','Why combine BCE with Dice loss?','BCE provides pixel-level binary learning, including background. The positive-pair Dice term emphasizes overlap on real defects. They are equally weighted. This is soft Dice on probabilities; training does not threshold the map first.'),
('training','Why did the M4 loss fall without foreground success?','Background is abundant, so learning to suppress output can reduce loss while still missing defects. M4 foreground Dice stayed zero and scores stayed near 0.300. I describe that as foreground underfitting, not successful convergence.'),
('training','What checkpoint is used after 25 EfficientNet epochs?','The checkpoint from epoch 19, which had the lowest recorded tuning loss. All 25 epochs are in the history, but inference reloads the saved best state rather than assuming the final epoch is best.'),
('training','What does AMP actually save?','It reduces suitable activation/operation precision and can lower GPU memory use and runtime. It does not simply halve FP32 weights, gradients and AdamW state. GradScaler handles gradient scaling; gradients are unscaled before clipping.'),
('training','Did we use gradient accumulation in the final project?','No. It appears as an M4 memory/step calculation. The final loop performs one optimizer update per batch of 16. Four micro-batches of eight would instead give effective batch 32 if implemented.'),
('evaluation','Why is all-empty naive Dice about 0.842?','Empty targets dominate. Under the empty-aware convention, an empty prediction gets Dice 1 on empty pairs and 0 on positive pairs. There are 38,371 empty pairs out of 45,572. The weighted score is only 0.300 because foreground Dice is zero.'),
('evaluation','How can pixel accuracy be 99.5% while masks are poor?','Most pixels are background. Correct background predictions dominate pixel accuracy, while missed rare defects can still matter greatly. Foreground Dice, class-level F1, empty accuracy and examples reveal problems hidden by accuracy.'),
('evaluation','Why did we keep the ensemble when TTA had a better audit score?','The selection used tuning, where the ensemble was best. Audit is a report on that frozen decision. Switching after inspecting the audit ranking would use audit to tune again. I acknowledge that the ensemble did not win on audit.'),
('evaluation','Is the audit set completely untouched across all project work?','No. It is half of the original M1 outer holdout, which earlier milestone work had already examined. It is held out from the new final selection decisions, rather than claimed to be entirely unseen throughout the whole project.'),
('implementation','What threshold comparison and connectivity did final inference use?','The exact final code uses probabilities >= the class threshold and scipy.ndimage.label default 4-connectivity. M4/M5 exercises use strict > and M5 asks for 8-connectivity. I do not interchange those descriptions.'),
('implementation','Why must you undo a TTA flip?','The augmented output is expressed in the transformed image frame. Inverse-flipping restores correspondence to the original pixels. Without it, averages mix different physical positions and can shift or blur defects.'),
('implementation','What order comes after the model outputs?','Sigmoid logits into probabilities; inverse-align any TTA views; average views and model probabilities; threshold; connected-component area filter; column-major RLE. Thresholding early throws away confidence, and RLE before filtering encodes an unfinished mask.'),
('implementation','Why does inference use eval() and inference_mode()?','eval() selects evaluation behavior for BatchNorm and dropout. inference_mode() disables autograd bookkeeping. They solve different problems; disabling gradients alone does not put a model into evaluation mode.'),
('evaluation','Does 0.67008 mean 67% pixel accuracy?','No. It is the recorded public Kaggle weighted score. Pixel accuracy, foreground Dice and class-macro pixel F1 are different reported quantities with different averaging.'),
('implementation','How is the deployed demo different from Gradio?','The final deployment is a static Hugging Face app using ONNX Runtime Web in the browser. The Gradio code remains an archived prototype. I should describe the runtime that was actually deployed.'),
('evaluation','Can minimum-area filtering remove a true defect?','Yes. In the full-data M5 exercise, an 8-connected 100-pixel filter removes every component of 48 genuinely positive image–class pairs. Defect C loses 24 pairs. This is a post-processing recall limit even for a perfect probability map.'),
('training','What would you improve next without leaderboard brute force?','Investigate rare-class and small-defect failures, run motivated controlled comparisons with a fixed validation protocol, and estimate variability across seeds. I would avoid changing many components together or selecting settings repeatedly on the public score.'),
('foundation','What do W&B logs prove and what do they not prove?','They record the configuration and measured history of real runs. Matching logs to CSVs and checkpoints supports traceability. Logs alone do not prove model quality, independent authorship or complete reproducibility on every device.')
]

